# templates/launchd/OWNED-BY-OTHER-INSTALLERS — launchd labels THIS REPO installs from a surface
# other than `scripts/install-launchd.sh`'s template set (#1867 review P1).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# `install-launchd.sh --status` reports any installed `com.eldato.*` / `com.tortoise.*` plist with no
# template as UNMANAGED and exits non-zero. That instrument is right, but its declared set is ONE
# directory, and this repo installs launchd jobs from a second surface: the harness controller
# `com.eldato.orchestrator-loop`, rendered by `scripts/orchestrator-harness/install.sh` from
# `scripts/orchestrator-harness/launchd.plist.template` and cycled through `launchd-cycle.sh` — it is
# deliberately NOT in this directory, because that installer owns its own lifecycle.
#
# Flagging it made `--status` exit 1 forever on the reference Mac layout, and the caller that gates on
# it hard-FAILs: `scripts/fleet-schedule.py` → `FAIL … SCHEDULE NOT VERIFIED` →
# `activate-harness.sh` → `fleet-schedule … FAILING`. A drift instrument whose healthy state is red
# is one an operator learns to ignore — or silences by deleting a plist the controller needs.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE
# -----------------
# A label that (a) matches the UNMANAGED scope, (b) is installed by another installer in THIS repo,
# and (c) therefore has no template here. A job that should be GONE belongs in `RETIRED` instead
# (that is a removal). A job nobody declares is exactly what UNMANAGED is for — do not add it here
# to quiet the alarm.
#
# ⛔ DRIFT-GATED: `scripts/install-launchd.test.sh` §18 asserts every entry below still matches the
# declaring installer's own `LABEL=` / `BASE_LABEL=` line, so this file cannot silently drift from
# the installer it describes. Remove an entry when its installer stops installing it.
#
#   com.eldato.orchestrator-loop   ← scripts/orchestrator-harness/install.sh (BASE_LABEL)
#                                   also scripts/orchestrator-bringup.sh (LABEL)
com.eldato.orchestrator-loop
